findings.body:
The React Scan loader logs the failure with `console.error` before falling back to an empty component. The TanStack loader swallows the same kind of failure with a bare `.catch(() => ...)` and no logging. The PR's stated goal is a consistent loader pattern, but a failed TanStack chunk in development now disappears with no trace, which makes it hard to tell why the devtools panel is missing.

::diff{file="web/app/components/devtools/tanstack/loader.tsx" lines="6-12"}

::callout{type="info"}
Use the same `.catch((error) => ...)` shape and `console.error` message as `react-scan/loader.tsx`, so both loaders behave the same way when a chunk fails to load.

The React Scan loader's `Suspense` fallback is a visible `<div>` containing the text 'Loading devtools...'. It is rendered as a direct child of `<body>`, before `ThemeProvider`, and stays in the DOM until the React Scan chunk loads. In development this shows as a stray line of text during initial load, and it can shift the layout. The actual component renders `null`, so the text is not needed for anything. The TanStack loader uses `fallback={null}`, so the two loaders are inconsistent here too.

